 "standard_url": "https://v9.australiancurriculum.edu.au/f-10-curriculum/learning-areas/mathematics",
 "standard_text": "use the 4 operations with integers and with rational numbers, choosing and using efficient strategies and digital tools where appropriate",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 8 maths worksheets on integers and rational numbers for the Australian Curriculum (AC9M8N04): multiplying and dividing integers, order of operations, and negative fractions and decimals. 100 questions with an answer key.",
 "key_big": "Same signs give a positive; different signs give a negative.",
 "key_sub": "Do the calculation without signs, then decide the sign. Use the order of operations.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>For × and ÷: <b>same signs give a positive, different signs give a negative</b>. With more than two numbers, an even number of negative signs gives a positive answer.</p><p>Use the order of operations: brackets, powers, × and ÷, then + and −. The same rules work for negative fractions and decimals: do the calculation without signs, then decide the sign.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Work out −6 × 4 and −24 ÷ (−3).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>6 × 4 = 24, different signs → −24.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>24 ÷ 3 = 8, same signs → 8.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: −24 and 8</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Work out −3 × 5 + 7.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Multiply first: −15.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>−15 + 7.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: −8</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Work out −<span class='fr'><span class='fn'>2</span><span class='fd'>3</span></span> × <span class='fr'><span class='fn'>3</span><span class='fd'>4</span></span>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2/3 × 3/4 = 6/12 = 1/2.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Different signs.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: −1/2</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Getting the sign wrong: −4 × −3 = 12, not −12.</li><li>Thinking (−3)² = −9. A negative times a negative is positive, so (−3)² = 9.</li><li>Working left to right instead of multiplying first: −3 × 5 + 7 = −8.</li><li>Forgetting that −3² means −(3²) = −9.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is a negative times a negative?",
   "a": "A positive number. For example −4 × −3 = 12."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I divide negative numbers?",
   "a": "Divide the numbers as normal, then use the sign rule: same signs give a positive, different signs give a negative."
  },
  {
   "q": "Is (−5)² the same as −5²?",
   "a": "No. (−5)² = 25 because the bracket squares −5. −5² means −(5²) = −25."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is a rational number?",
   "a": "Any number that can be written as a fraction of two integers, such as −3/4, 0.6 or −5."
  }
 ],
